; SUBTRACTION AND DIVISION-BY-A-WORD ON THE FIXED-LIMB RUNG.
;
; Until this the limb library had `+`, `*` and nothing else, so a program that
; subtracted fell back to the host's own bignum — and on windows, which has
; none, it was refused. That is ADR 0019 item 4 half delivered: a target with
; arbitrary-precision arithmetic that could add and multiply and not subtract.
;
; WHAT MAKES THIS A REAL TEST is `; big-repr: both`. The same source is compiled
; twice on every target that can render the result: once storing the value in
; fixed limbs, where the subtraction is our own borrow chain and the division is
; our own one-pass `div-small`, and once storing it in the host's bignum, where
; both are `math/big`, `BigInt` and `BigInteger`. So the oracle is three mature
; host implementations rather than another copy of ours.
;
; The expected digits were computed independently and are below, because
; agreement is not correctness: a bug in the promotion would be shared by all
; six variants.
;
; skip: windows
;
; windows is skipped and the reason is a CAPABILITY, not a disagreement: it can
; now do this arithmetic and still cannot print the answer. `big-str` needs a
; host bignum to convert through and that target declares none, so rendering
; waits on decimal conversion from limbs, which waits on a string surface
; (docs/spec/strings.md). The arithmetic reaching that host at all is the point
; of the change this case tests.
;
; big-repr: both

; expect: 216049380697530863994753086400 246913577925925925692592592570 277777775154320987390432098740 308641972382716049088271604910 370370366839506172483950617250 462962958524691357577469135760 617283944666666666066666666610 864197522493827159649382715970 1265432086462962961721296296180

(export calc)

(sig calc ((n (int 0 100))) (int 0 (pow 2 300)))

(def calc (fn (n)
  (let (* 123456789012345678901234567890 (+ n 7)) (fn (a)
    (let (* 98765432109876543210 (+ n 3)) (fn (b)
      (/ (- a b) 4)))))))

(def run (fn (n) (big-str (calc n))))
